# Connection pools are sized EXPLICITLY, not inherited from a CPU count.
#
# Prisma's default is `num_physical_cpus * 2 + 1`, evaluated wherever the process happens to
# run. That made the pool 9 on a 4-core laptop and would make it 3 or 5 in an API container
# limited to one or two cores — a property the whole application depends on, decided silently
# by the environment. Measured 2026-09-21: concurrency reached 9 during a SERIAL test suite,
# i.e. it pinned at the pool size, so real demand is at least 9 and not otherwise known.
#
# The arithmetic, against Postgres `max_connections = 100`:
#
#   100 max_connections
# -   3 superuser_reserved_connections (Postgres default)
# =  97 usable
#
#   15 app pool (APP_DATABASE_URL — every ordinary request)
# +  5 owner pool (DATABASE_URL — identity/bootstrap, migrations, seed; low concurrency)
# =  20 per API process  ->  up to 4 instances within 97
#
# If more than four API instances are ever run, RAISE max_connections or LOWER these — do not
# discover the ceiling during an incident. Revisit at the deployment-target decision, which is
# also when the instance count stops being hypothetical.
# Copy to .env.uat. Points every UAT-only command at the db-uat container
# instead of the dev database in .env or the test database in .env.test —
# see docker-compose.yml's db-uat service. Neither the dev nor the test
# database is ever read or written by anything that loads this file.
#
# Backlog A.10 (Part 10.4/10.5): "Separate Dev/Test/UAT/Prod environments +
# masked/synthesized production data in non-production environments." No
# npm script reads this file yet — same "no real deployment target" gap as
# the rest of this repo's environment story (see README § Deployment). It
# exists so the day a UAT deploy target is chosen, its config isn't invented
# from scratch, and so this file's own existence is a standing reminder that
# UAT must never be seeded from a raw production export — only from
# synthesizeEntityFields()-processed data (apps/api/src/modules/security/
# synthetic-data.util.ts) or hand-built fixtures.

POSTGRES_USER=ibms
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=ibms
POSTGRES_DB=ibms_uat

# Host-side port — dev is 5433, test is 5434, CI's ephemeral container is
# 5432; UAT gets 5435 so all four can run at once without colliding.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://ibms:ibms@localhost:5435/ibms_uat?schema=public&connection_limit=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-tenancy Phase 2 step 8 — the RUNTIME database connection.
#
# The API connects as a NON-OWNER role so that PostgreSQL applies the row-level
# security policies to it. This is not optional hardening: Postgres exempts a
# table's OWNER from that table's own policies, so connecting the API as the
# migration role above would leave all 118 policies silently, completely inert
# while looking perfectly configured.
#
# DATABASE_URL (above) stays the OWNER connection and is used only by
# `prisma migrate` and the seed — both legitimately need to reach across every
# Organization at once.
#
# Create the role first:  APP_DB_PASSWORD=... npm run db:provision-app-role
# Then run the migrations, which GRANT to it and install the policies.
#
# If this is unset the API still boots, but logs a loud error and runs with
# tenant isolation resting on the application layer alone.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
APP_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://ibms_app:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5435/ibms_uat?schema=public&connection_limit=15

# --- apps/api auth (Part 10.1) --- see .env.example for what these are.
# UAT-only example values below — generate real secrets per environment,
# never reuse these, and never reuse a Dev/Test/Prod secret here either.
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=uat-only-change-me-32-characters-minimum
MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=jWvaaenpb1O7k9jMlLZhRTeQFNJ1jQOFTUHtzgTHNtg=

# --- apps/api field-level encryption (Part 10.2) --- see .env.example.
PII_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=v1:DzVvLT8z/wNB6UZW2cglf0EEajI/+hjMXk8WlVfHphI=
PII_ENCRYPTION_ACTIVE_KEY_ID=v1

# UAT is a non-production environment used by real testers, not just
# automation — leave this off unless a real email/notification provider is
# wired for it, to avoid a raw reset token showing up somewhere a human
# could screenshot it.
ENABLE_DEV_RESET_TOKEN=false

# --- apps/web ---
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000
